cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: ".claude: the agent configuration for the multiplayer-fabric workspace"
abstract: >
  Claude Code's workspace configuration, kept as a repo project checked out at
  .claude/ rather than as a symlink or a file on one machine. Settings that decide
  what an agent may do without asking are tracked, reviewed and visible to repo
  status, so a widened permission arrives as a diff with a commit behind it. The
  per-machine half stays untracked. The conventions it works alongside are in
  infrastructure-logbook; this repository holds capability rather than convention.
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 0.1.0-dev.1
date-released: "2026-08-17"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/.claude"
keywords:
  - claude-code
  - agent-configuration
  - repo-manifest

# Configuration for a tool, so what it is built on is the tool's format rather than
# any code. There is nothing vendored and nothing linked.
references:

  - type: software
    title: "Claude Code"
    authors:
      - name: Anthropic
    url: "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code"
    notes: >
      The agent this configures. It reads `.claude/settings.json` and merges
      `settings.local.json` over it with local winning, which is why the two are
      separate files and why only the first is tracked.

  - type: software
    title: "repo: a tool built on top of Git for managing many repositories"
    authors:
      - name: "The Android Open Source Project"
    url: "https://gerrit.googlesource.com/git-repo"
    notes: >
      What puts this at `.claude/`. `_CheckLocalPath` bans only `.`, `..`, `.git` and
      `.repo*` as path components, so a leading-dot path is legal and gets an ordinary
      managed checkout with its gitdir under `.repo/projects/`.

  - type: software
    title: "infrastructure-logbook: the fabric's record of itself"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/infrastructure-logbook"
    notes: >
      The other half. It holds the conventions and the gates; this holds what an agent
      may do without asking. The settings lived there until they were split out, because
      a symlink into it would have made one repository's permissions everybody's with
      nothing able to see it.
